{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The tool approval flow allows you to require user approval before executing sensitive tools, giving users control over actions like sending emails, making purchases, or deleting data. A tool call moves through the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ToolCallState"},{"type":"text","value":" lifecycle:"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The current client API exposes approvals as bound AG-UI interrupts. For the\ncomplete server/client lifecycle, atomic batch controls, generic interrupts,\nand recovery, see "},{"type":"link","href":"../interrupts/overview","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Interrupts"}]},{"type":"text","value":". For deprecated API mapping,\nsee "},{"type":"link","href":"../interrupts/migration","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Migrate to AG-UI interrupts"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"awaiting-input"}]},{"type":"text","value":" — Tool call started, no arguments yet"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"input-streaming"}]},{"type":"text","value":" — Arguments arriving incrementally"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"input-complete"}]},{"type":"text","value":" — All arguments received"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"approval-requested"}]},{"type":"text","value":" — Waiting for user approval (only if "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"needsApproval: true"},{"type":"text","value":")"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"approval-responded"}]},{"type":"text","value":" — User approved or denied"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"After "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"approval-responded"},{"type":"text","value":" the call executes (if approved). Although "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"complete"},{"type":"text","value":" exists in the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ToolCallState"},{"type":"text","value":" union, the runtime never transitions the tool-call part to it — the result surfaces as a populated "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"part.output"},{"type":"text","value":" plus a sibling "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tool-result"},{"type":"text","value":" part whose own state is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"complete"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"error"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Approvals run ephemerally: the run resumes from the full client message\nhistory that the browser sends back, so a stateless route needs no server\nstorage to rebuild the paused call."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When a tool requires approval, the typical flow is:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Model calls the tool"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tool execution is paused"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"User is prompted to approve or deny"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tool executes (if approved) or produces a denied/cancelled result"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Conversation continues with the result"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"resolve-an-approval-interrupt","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Resolve an approval interrupt"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Without an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"approvalSchema"},{"type":"text","value":", use the boolean shorthand. Approval uses the\noriginal tool input by default:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const approval = interrupts.find(\n  (interrupt) => interrupt.kind === 'tool-approval',\n)\n\nif (approval?.kind === 'tool-approval') {\n  approval.resolveInterrupt(true)\n}","lang":"ts","meta":"ignore"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"approvalSchema"},{"type":"text","value":" can define separate application payloads for approval and\nrejection:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { toolDefinition } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\n\nconst transferDefinition = toolDefinition({\n  name: 'transfer',\n  description: 'Transfer funds',\n  needsApproval: true,\n  inputSchema: z.object({\n    amount: z.number().positive(),\n    recipient: z.string(),\n  }),\n  approvalSchema: {\n    approve: z.object({ note: z.string() }),\n    reject: z.object({ reason: z.string() }),\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep branch data under "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"payload"},{"type":"text","value":". Approved arguments can optionally be replaced\nin full with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"editedArgs"},{"type":"text","value":"; rejection never accepts edits:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"approval.resolveInterrupt(true, {\n  editedArgs: { amount: 12, recipient: 'Ada' },\n  payload: { note: 'Reviewed' },\n})\n\napproval.resolveInterrupt(false, {\n  payload: { reason: 'Policy limit' },\n})","lang":"ts","meta":"ignore"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Denial and cancellation are different. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resolveInterrupt(false, ...)"},{"type":"text","value":" records a\nresolved rejection for the continuation. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cancel()"},{"type":"text","value":" is payloadless and\ndoes not select the reject schema:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"approval.cancel()","lang":"ts","meta":"ignore"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A singleton submits after its valid resolution. Multiple items stage until all\nare valid, then submit atomically. Use root "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resolveInterrupts(...)"},{"type":"text","value":" for one\nsynchronous batch transaction. See "},{"type":"link","href":"../interrupts/multiple","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Multiple Interrupts"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"enabling-approval","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Enabling Approval"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tools can be marked as requiring approval by setting "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"needsApproval: true"},{"type":"text","value":" in the definition:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { toolDefinition } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\nimport { emailService } from './email-service'\n\n// Step 1: Define tool with approval requirement\nconst sendEmailDef = toolDefinition({\n  name: 'send_email',\n  description: 'Send an email to a recipient',\n  inputSchema: z.object({\n    to: z.string().email(),\n    subject: z.string(),\n    body: z.string(),\n  }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({\n    success: z.boolean(),\n    messageId: z.string(),\n  }),\n  needsApproval: true, // This tool requires approval\n})\n\n// Step 2: Create server implementation\nconst sendEmail = sendEmailDef.server(async ({ to, subject, body }) => {\n  // Only executes if approved\n  await emailService.send({ to, subject, body })\n  return { success: true, messageId: '...' }\n})","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"server-side-approval","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Server-Side Approval"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"On the server, tools with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"needsApproval: true"},{"type":"text","value":" will pause execution and wait for approval:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { chat, toServerSentEventsResponse } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport { sendEmail } from './tools'\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { messages } = await request.json()\n\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n    messages,\n    tools: [sendEmail],\n  })\n\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream)\n}","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"approval-ui","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Approval UI"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Render pending approvals from the hook's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"interrupts"},{"type":"text","value":" array. Each\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tool-approval"},{"type":"text","value":" interrupt carries the tool name, the original arguments, and a\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resolveInterrupt"},{"type":"text","value":" you call with the user's decision. The array is already\ntool-agnostic, so one block handles every tool marked "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"needsApproval: true"},{"type":"text","value":" —\nno per-tool "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"part.name"},{"type":"text","value":" branch and no reading "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"part.approval"},{"type":"text","value":" off a mixed union:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { useChat, fetchServerSentEvents } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\nimport { sendEmail } from './tools'\n\nfunction ChatComponent() {\n  const { messages, sendMessage, interrupts, resuming } = useChat({\n    connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n    tools: [sendEmail],\n  })\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n      {/* ...render messages... */}\n      {interrupts.map((interrupt) =>\n        interrupt.kind === 'tool-approval' ? (\n          <div key={interrupt.id} className=\"approval-prompt\">\n            <p>🔒 Approve {interrupt.toolName}?</p>\n            <pre>{JSON.stringify(interrupt.originalArgs, null, 2)}</pre>\n            <button\n              disabled={!interrupt.canResolve || resuming}\n              onClick={() => interrupt.resolveInterrupt(true)}\n            >\n              Approve\n            </button>\n            <button\n              disabled={!interrupt.canResolve || resuming}\n              onClick={() => interrupt.resolveInterrupt(false)}\n            >\n              Deny\n            </button>\n          </div>\n        ) : null,\n      )}\n    </div>\n  )\n}","lang":"tsx","meta":"ignore"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"canResolve"},{"type":"text","value":" stays "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"},{"type":"text","value":" until the interrupt is bound and ready; "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resuming"},{"type":"text","value":" is\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":" while a resolution is in flight, so gate the buttons on both."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"migrating-from-addtoolapprovalresponse","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Migrating from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"addToolApprovalResponse"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Older UIs read "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"part.approval"},{"type":"text","value":" off tool-call parts and called\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"addToolApprovalResponse({ id, approved })"},{"type":"text","value":". That API is deprecated. Render from\nthe "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"interrupts"},{"type":"text","value":" array and call "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resolveInterrupt"},{"type":"text","value":" instead (see "},{"type":"link","href":"#approval-ui","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Approval\nUI"}]},{"type":"text","value":" above) — it is tool-agnostic by default, so the per-tool\nnarrowing the part-based pattern needed goes away. For the full mapping, see\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../interrupts/migration","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Migrate to AG-UI interrupts"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"client-tools-with-approval","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client Tools with Approval"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client tools can also require approval:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { toolDefinition } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\nimport { useChat, fetchServerSentEvents } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\n\n// tools/definitions.ts\nconst deleteLocalDataDef = toolDefinition({\n  name: 'delete_local_data',\n  description: 'Delete data from local storage',\n  inputSchema: z.object({\n    key: z.string(),\n  }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({\n    deleted: z.boolean(),\n  }),\n  needsApproval: true, // Requires approval even on client\n})\n\n// Client: Create implementation\nconst deleteLocalData = deleteLocalDataDef.client((input) => {\n  // This will only execute after approval\n  localStorage.removeItem(input.key)\n  return { deleted: true }\n})\n\nconst { messages, interrupts } = useChat({\n  connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n  // Pass client tools as a plain array — literal tool-name inference works\n  // without a wrapper. The approval surfaces as a `tool-approval` interrupt you\n  // resolve from `interrupts` (see Approval UI); the tool runs on approval.\n  tools: [deleteLocalData], // Automatic execution after approval\n})","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"example-e-commerce-purchase","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Example: E-commerce Purchase"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { toolDefinition } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\nimport { createOrder } from './orders'\n\n// Define tool with approval requirement\nconst purchaseItemDef = toolDefinition({\n  name: 'purchase_item',\n  description: 'Purchase an item from the store',\n  inputSchema: z.object({\n    itemId: z.string(),\n    quantity: z.number(),\n    price: z.number(),\n  }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({\n    orderId: z.string(),\n    total: z.number(),\n  }),\n  needsApproval: true,\n})\n\n// Create server implementation\nconst purchaseItem = purchaseItemDef.server(\n  async ({ itemId, quantity, price }) => {\n    const order = await createOrder({ itemId, quantity, price })\n    return { orderId: order.id, total: price * quantity }\n  },\n)","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The user will see an approval prompt showing the item, quantity, and price before the purchase is made. The tool will only execute after the user approves."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"best-practices","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Best Practices"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use approval for sensitive operations"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Sending emails, making payments, deleting data"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Show clear information"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Display what the tool will do before approval"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Provide context"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Show tool arguments in a readable format"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Handle denial gracefully"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Check "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"part.outcome"},{"type":"text","value":" when a tool is denied."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Timeout handling"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Consider timeouts for approval requests"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"tool-result-outcomes","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tool Result Outcomes"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cancelled and denied tool results keep the existing "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state: 'error'"},{"type":"text","value":" and wire\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"output-error"},{"type":"text","value":" behavior for compatibility, but "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ToolResultPart.outcome"},{"type":"text","value":" is set\nto "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'cancelled'"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'denied'"},{"type":"text","value":". Ordinary tool execution failures do not set an\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"outcome"},{"type":"text","value":". The outcome can be produced by the user decision or by middleware\npolicy."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When a result is persisted as a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ModelMessage"},{"type":"text","value":", the outcome is stored in\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"metadata.tanstack.toolResultOutcome"},{"type":"text","value":" and restored to the UI part. Consumers\nshould use this structured value instead of matching the fixed error text:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const denied = message.parts.some(\n  (part) => part.type === 'tool-result' && part.outcome === 'denied',\n)","lang":"tsx","meta":"ignore"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-client"},{"type":"text","value":" package exposes the same value on the\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tool-result"},{"type":"text","value":" part. Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"part.outcome"},{"type":"text","value":" to distinguish a denial or cancellation\nfrom an ordinary tool execution failure."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The structured outcome is emitted by both the AG-UI interrupt resume path and\nthe deprecated "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"addToolApprovalResponse"},{"type":"text","value":" path. Both paths keep the existing\nerror content."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"next-steps","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Next Steps"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./server-tools","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Server Tools"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Learn about server-side tool execution"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./client-tools","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client Tools"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Learn about client-side tool execution"}]}]}]}],"headings":[{"id":"resolve-an-approval-interrupt","text":"Resolve an approval interrupt","level":2},{"id":"enabling-approval","text":"Enabling Approval","level":2},{"id":"server-side-approval","text":"Server-Side Approval","level":2},{"id":"approval-ui","text":"Approval UI","level":2},{"id":"migrating-from-addtoolapprovalresponse","text":"Migrating from addToolApprovalResponse","level":2},{"id":"client-tools-with-approval","text":"Client Tools with Approval","level":2},{"id":"example-e-commerce-purchase","text":"Example: E-commerce Purchase","level":2},{"id":"best-practices","text":"Best Practices","level":2},{"id":"tool-result-outcomes","text":"Tool Result Outcomes","level":2},{"id":"next-steps","text":"Next Steps","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nThe tool approval flow allows you to require user approval before executing sensitive tools, giving users control over actions like sending emails, making purchases, or deleting data. A tool call moves through the `ToolCallState` lifecycle:\n\nThe current client API exposes approvals as bound AG-UI interrupts. For the\ncomplete server/client lifecycle, atomic batch controls, generic interrupts,\nand recovery, see [Interrupts](../interrupts/overview). For deprecated API mapping,\nsee [Migrate to AG-UI interrupts](../interrupts/migration).\n\n1. **`awaiting-input`** — Tool call started, no arguments yet\n2. **`input-streaming`** — Arguments arriving incrementally\n3. **`input-complete`** — All arguments received\n4. **`approval-requested`** — Waiting for user approval (only if `needsApproval: true`)\n5. **`approval-responded`** — User approved or denied\n\nAfter `approval-responded` the call executes (if approved). Although `complete` exists in the `ToolCallState` union, the runtime never transitions the tool-call part to it — the result surfaces as a populated `part.output` plus a sibling `tool-result` part whose own state is `complete` or `error`.\n\nApprovals run ephemerally: the run resumes from the full client message\nhistory that the browser sends back, so a stateless route needs no server\nstorage to rebuild the paused call.\n\nWhen a tool requires approval, the typical flow is:\n\n1. Model calls the tool\n2. Tool execution is paused\n3. User is prompted to approve or deny\n4. Tool executes (if approved) or produces a denied/cancelled result\n5. Conversation continues with the result\n\n## Resolve an approval interrupt\n\nWithout an `approvalSchema`, use the boolean shorthand. Approval uses the\noriginal tool input by default:\n\n```ts ignore\nconst approval = interrupts.find(\n  (interrupt) => interrupt.kind === 'tool-approval',\n)\n\nif (approval?.kind === 'tool-approval') {\n  approval.resolveInterrupt(true)\n}\n```\n\nAn `approvalSchema` can define separate application payloads for approval and\nrejection:\n\n```ts\nimport { toolDefinition } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\n\nconst transferDefinition = toolDefinition({\n  name: 'transfer',\n  description: 'Transfer funds',\n  needsApproval: true,\n  inputSchema: z.object({\n    amount: z.number().positive(),\n    recipient: z.string(),\n  }),\n  approvalSchema: {\n    approve: z.object({ note: z.string() }),\n    reject: z.object({ reason: z.string() }),\n  },\n})\n```\n\nKeep branch data under `payload`. Approved arguments can optionally be replaced\nin full with `editedArgs`; rejection never accepts edits:\n\n```ts ignore\napproval.resolveInterrupt(true, {\n  editedArgs: { amount: 12, recipient: 'Ada' },\n  payload: { note: 'Reviewed' },\n})\n\napproval.resolveInterrupt(false, {\n  payload: { reason: 'Policy limit' },\n})\n```\n\nDenial and cancellation are different. `resolveInterrupt(false, ...)` records a\nresolved rejection for the continuation. `cancel()` is payloadless and\ndoes not select the reject schema:\n\n```ts ignore\napproval.cancel()\n```\n\nA singleton submits after its valid resolution. Multiple items stage until all\nare valid, then submit atomically. Use root `resolveInterrupts(...)` for one\nsynchronous batch transaction. See [Multiple Interrupts](../interrupts/multiple).\n\n## Enabling Approval\n\nTools can be marked as requiring approval by setting `needsApproval: true` in the definition:\n\n```typescript\nimport { toolDefinition } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\nimport { emailService } from './email-service'\n\n// Step 1: Define tool with approval requirement\nconst sendEmailDef = toolDefinition({\n  name: 'send_email',\n  description: 'Send an email to a recipient',\n  inputSchema: z.object({\n    to: z.string().email(),\n    subject: z.string(),\n    body: z.string(),\n  }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({\n    success: z.boolean(),\n    messageId: z.string(),\n  }),\n  needsApproval: true, // This tool requires approval\n})\n\n// Step 2: Create server implementation\nconst sendEmail = sendEmailDef.server(async ({ to, subject, body }) => {\n  // Only executes if approved\n  await emailService.send({ to, subject, body })\n  return { success: true, messageId: '...' }\n})\n```\n\n## Server-Side Approval\n\nOn the server, tools with `needsApproval: true` will pause execution and wait for approval:\n\n```typescript\nimport { chat, toServerSentEventsResponse } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport { sendEmail } from './tools'\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { messages } = await request.json()\n\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n    messages,\n    tools: [sendEmail],\n  })\n\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream)\n}\n```\n\n## Approval UI\n\nRender pending approvals from the hook's `interrupts` array. Each\n`tool-approval` interrupt carries the tool name, the original arguments, and a\n`resolveInterrupt` you call with the user's decision. The array is already\ntool-agnostic, so one block handles every tool marked `needsApproval: true` —\nno per-tool `part.name` branch and no reading `part.approval` off a mixed union:\n\n```tsx ignore\nimport { useChat, fetchServerSentEvents } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\nimport { sendEmail } from './tools'\n\nfunction ChatComponent() {\n  const { messages, sendMessage, interrupts, resuming } = useChat({\n    connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n    tools: [sendEmail],\n  })\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n      {/* ...render messages... */}\n      {interrupts.map((interrupt) =>\n        interrupt.kind === 'tool-approval' ? (\n          <div key={interrupt.id} className=\"approval-prompt\">\n            <p>🔒 Approve {interrupt.toolName}?</p>\n            <pre>{JSON.stringify(interrupt.originalArgs, null, 2)}</pre>\n            <button\n              disabled={!interrupt.canResolve || resuming}\n              onClick={() => interrupt.resolveInterrupt(true)}\n            >\n              Approve\n            </button>\n            <button\n              disabled={!interrupt.canResolve || resuming}\n              onClick={() => interrupt.resolveInterrupt(false)}\n            >\n              Deny\n            </button>\n          </div>\n        ) : null,\n      )}\n    </div>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n`canResolve` stays `false` until the interrupt is bound and ready; `resuming` is\n`true` while a resolution is in flight, so gate the buttons on both.\n\n## Migrating from `addToolApprovalResponse`\n\nOlder UIs read `part.approval` off tool-call parts and called\n`addToolApprovalResponse({ id, approved })`. That API is deprecated. Render from\nthe `interrupts` array and call `resolveInterrupt` instead (see [Approval\nUI](#approval-ui) above) — it is tool-agnostic by default, so the per-tool\nnarrowing the part-based pattern needed goes away. For the full mapping, see\n[Migrate to AG-UI interrupts](../interrupts/migration).\n\n## Client Tools with Approval\n\nClient tools can also require approval:\n\n```typescript\nimport { toolDefinition } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\nimport { useChat, fetchServerSentEvents } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\n\n// tools/definitions.ts\nconst deleteLocalDataDef = toolDefinition({\n  name: 'delete_local_data',\n  description: 'Delete data from local storage',\n  inputSchema: z.object({\n    key: z.string(),\n  }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({\n    deleted: z.boolean(),\n  }),\n  needsApproval: true, // Requires approval even on client\n})\n\n// Client: Create implementation\nconst deleteLocalData = deleteLocalDataDef.client((input) => {\n  // This will only execute after approval\n  localStorage.removeItem(input.key)\n  return { deleted: true }\n})\n\nconst { messages, interrupts } = useChat({\n  connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n  // Pass client tools as a plain array — literal tool-name inference works\n  // without a wrapper. The approval surfaces as a `tool-approval` interrupt you\n  // resolve from `interrupts` (see Approval UI); the tool runs on approval.\n  tools: [deleteLocalData], // Automatic execution after approval\n})\n```\n\n## Example: E-commerce Purchase\n\n```typescript\nimport { toolDefinition } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\nimport { createOrder } from './orders'\n\n// Define tool with approval requirement\nconst purchaseItemDef = toolDefinition({\n  name: 'purchase_item',\n  description: 'Purchase an item from the store',\n  inputSchema: z.object({\n    itemId: z.string(),\n    quantity: z.number(),\n    price: z.number(),\n  }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({\n    orderId: z.string(),\n    total: z.number(),\n  }),\n  needsApproval: true,\n})\n\n// Create server implementation\nconst purchaseItem = purchaseItemDef.server(\n  async ({ itemId, quantity, price }) => {\n    const order = await createOrder({ itemId, quantity, price })\n    return { orderId: order.id, total: price * quantity }\n  },\n)\n```\n\nThe user will see an approval prompt showing the item, quantity, and price before the purchase is made. The tool will only execute after the user approves.\n\n## Best Practices\n\n- **Use approval for sensitive operations** - Sending emails, making payments, deleting data\n- **Show clear information** - Display what the tool will do before approval\n- **Provide context** - Show tool arguments in a readable format\n- **Handle denial gracefully** - Check `part.outcome` when a tool is denied.\n- **Timeout handling** - Consider timeouts for approval requests\n\n## Tool Result Outcomes\n\nCancelled and denied tool results keep the existing `state: 'error'` and wire\n`output-error` behavior for compatibility, but `ToolResultPart.outcome` is set\nto `'cancelled'` or `'denied'`. Ordinary tool execution failures do not set an\n`outcome`. The outcome can be produced by the user decision or by middleware\npolicy.\n\nWhen a result is persisted as a `ModelMessage`, the outcome is stored in\n`metadata.tanstack.toolResultOutcome` and restored to the UI part. Consumers\nshould use this structured value instead of matching the fixed error text:\n\n```tsx ignore\nconst denied = message.parts.some(\n  (part) => part.type === 'tool-result' && part.outcome === 'denied',\n)\n```\n\nThe `@tanstack/ai-client` package exposes the same value on the\n`tool-result` part. Use `part.outcome` to distinguish a denial or cancellation\nfrom an ordinary tool execution failure.\n\nThe structured outcome is emitted by both the AG-UI interrupt resume path and\nthe deprecated `addToolApprovalResponse` path. Both paths keep the existing\nerror content.\n\n## Next Steps\n\n- [Server Tools](./server-tools) - Learn about server-side tool execution\n- [Client Tools](./client-tools) - Learn about client-side tool execution\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Tool Approval Flow\nid: tool-approval-flow\norder: 5\ndescription: 'Require user approval before executing sensitive tools in TanStack AI — approval states, deny flows, and batched approvals with needsApproval.'\nkeywords:\n  - tanstack ai\n  - tool approval\n  - needsApproval\n  - user consent\n  - sensitive tools\n  - approval flow\n  - human-in-the-loop\n---\n\nThe tool approval flow allows you to require user approval before executing sensitive tools, giving users control over actions like sending emails, making purchases, or deleting data. A tool call moves through the `ToolCallState` lifecycle:\n\nThe current client API exposes approvals as bound AG-UI interrupts. For the\ncomplete server/client lifecycle, atomic batch controls, generic interrupts,\nand recovery, see [Interrupts](../interrupts/overview). For deprecated API mapping,\nsee [Migrate to AG-UI interrupts](../interrupts/migration).\n\n1. **`awaiting-input`** — Tool call started, no arguments yet\n2. **`input-streaming`** — Arguments arriving incrementally\n3. **`input-complete`** — All arguments received\n4. **`approval-requested`** — Waiting for user approval (only if `needsApproval: true`)\n5. **`approval-responded`** — User approved or denied\n\nAfter `approval-responded` the call executes (if approved). Although `complete` exists in the `ToolCallState` union, the runtime never transitions the tool-call part to it — the result surfaces as a populated `part.output` plus a sibling `tool-result` part whose own state is `complete` or `error`.\n\nApprovals run ephemerally: the run resumes from the full client message\nhistory that the browser sends back, so a stateless route needs no server\nstorage to rebuild the paused call.\n\nWhen a tool requires approval, the typical flow is:\n\n1. Model calls the tool\n2. Tool execution is paused\n3. User is prompted to approve or deny\n4. Tool executes (if approved) or produces a denied/cancelled result\n5. Conversation continues with the result\n\n## Resolve an approval interrupt\n\nWithout an `approvalSchema`, use the boolean shorthand. Approval uses the\noriginal tool input by default:\n\n```ts ignore\nconst approval = interrupts.find(\n  (interrupt) => interrupt.kind === 'tool-approval',\n)\n\nif (approval?.kind === 'tool-approval') {\n  approval.resolveInterrupt(true)\n}\n```\n\nAn `approvalSchema` can define separate application payloads for approval and\nrejection:\n\n```ts\nimport { toolDefinition } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\n\nconst transferDefinition = toolDefinition({\n  name: 'transfer',\n  description: 'Transfer funds',\n  needsApproval: true,\n  inputSchema: z.object({\n    amount: z.number().positive(),\n    recipient: z.string(),\n  }),\n  approvalSchema: {\n    approve: z.object({ note: z.string() }),\n    reject: z.object({ reason: z.string() }),\n  },\n})\n```\n\nKeep branch data under `payload`. Approved arguments can optionally be replaced\nin full with `editedArgs`; rejection never accepts edits:\n\n```ts ignore\napproval.resolveInterrupt(true, {\n  editedArgs: { amount: 12, recipient: 'Ada' },\n  payload: { note: 'Reviewed' },\n})\n\napproval.resolveInterrupt(false, {\n  payload: { reason: 'Policy limit' },\n})\n```\n\nDenial and cancellation are different. `resolveInterrupt(false, ...)` records a\nresolved rejection for the continuation. `cancel()` is payloadless and\ndoes not select the reject schema:\n\n```ts ignore\napproval.cancel()\n```\n\nA singleton submits after its valid resolution. Multiple items stage until all\nare valid, then submit atomically. Use root `resolveInterrupts(...)` for one\nsynchronous batch transaction. See [Multiple Interrupts](../interrupts/multiple).\n\n## Enabling Approval\n\nTools can be marked as requiring approval by setting `needsApproval: true` in the definition:\n\n```typescript\nimport { toolDefinition } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\nimport { emailService } from './email-service'\n\n// Step 1: Define tool with approval requirement\nconst sendEmailDef = toolDefinition({\n  name: 'send_email',\n  description: 'Send an email to a recipient',\n  inputSchema: z.object({\n    to: z.string().email(),\n    subject: z.string(),\n    body: z.string(),\n  }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({\n    success: z.boolean(),\n    messageId: z.string(),\n  }),\n  needsApproval: true, // This tool requires approval\n})\n\n// Step 2: Create server implementation\nconst sendEmail = sendEmailDef.server(async ({ to, subject, body }) => {\n  // Only executes if approved\n  await emailService.send({ to, subject, body })\n  return { success: true, messageId: '...' }\n})\n```\n\n## Server-Side Approval\n\nOn the server, tools with `needsApproval: true` will pause execution and wait for approval:\n\n```typescript\nimport { chat, toServerSentEventsResponse } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport { sendEmail } from './tools'\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { messages } = await request.json()\n\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n    messages,\n    tools: [sendEmail],\n  })\n\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream)\n}\n```\n\n## Approval UI\n\nRender pending approvals from the hook's `interrupts` array. Each\n`tool-approval` interrupt carries the tool name, the original arguments, and a\n`resolveInterrupt` you call with the user's decision. The array is already\ntool-agnostic, so one block handles every tool marked `needsApproval: true` —\nno per-tool `part.name` branch and no reading `part.approval` off a mixed union:\n\n```tsx ignore\nimport { useChat, fetchServerSentEvents } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\nimport { sendEmail } from './tools'\n\nfunction ChatComponent() {\n  const { messages, sendMessage, interrupts, resuming } = useChat({\n    connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n    tools: [sendEmail],\n  })\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n      {/* ...render messages... */}\n      {interrupts.map((interrupt) =>\n        interrupt.kind === 'tool-approval' ? (\n          <div key={interrupt.id} className=\"approval-prompt\">\n            <p>🔒 Approve {interrupt.toolName}?</p>\n            <pre>{JSON.stringify(interrupt.originalArgs, null, 2)}</pre>\n            <button\n              disabled={!interrupt.canResolve || resuming}\n              onClick={() => interrupt.resolveInterrupt(true)}\n            >\n              Approve\n            </button>\n            <button\n              disabled={!interrupt.canResolve || resuming}\n              onClick={() => interrupt.resolveInterrupt(false)}\n            >\n              Deny\n            </button>\n          </div>\n        ) : null,\n      )}\n    </div>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n`canResolve` stays `false` until the interrupt is bound and ready; `resuming` is\n`true` while a resolution is in flight, so gate the buttons on both.\n\n## Migrating from `addToolApprovalResponse`\n\nOlder UIs read `part.approval` off tool-call parts and called\n`addToolApprovalResponse({ id, approved })`. That API is deprecated. Render from\nthe `interrupts` array and call `resolveInterrupt` instead (see [Approval\nUI](#approval-ui) above) — it is tool-agnostic by default, so the per-tool\nnarrowing the part-based pattern needed goes away. For the full mapping, see\n[Migrate to AG-UI interrupts](../interrupts/migration).\n\n## Client Tools with Approval\n\nClient tools can also require approval:\n\n```typescript\nimport { toolDefinition } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\nimport { useChat, fetchServerSentEvents } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\n\n// tools/definitions.ts\nconst deleteLocalDataDef = toolDefinition({\n  name: 'delete_local_data',\n  description: 'Delete data from local storage',\n  inputSchema: z.object({\n    key: z.string(),\n  }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({\n    deleted: z.boolean(),\n  }),\n  needsApproval: true, // Requires approval even on client\n})\n\n// Client: Create implementation\nconst deleteLocalData = deleteLocalDataDef.client((input) => {\n  // This will only execute after approval\n  localStorage.removeItem(input.key)\n  return { deleted: true }\n})\n\nconst { messages, interrupts } = useChat({\n  connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n  // Pass client tools as a plain array — literal tool-name inference works\n  // without a wrapper. The approval surfaces as a `tool-approval` interrupt you\n  // resolve from `interrupts` (see Approval UI); the tool runs on approval.\n  tools: [deleteLocalData], // Automatic execution after approval\n})\n```\n\n## Example: E-commerce Purchase\n\n```typescript\nimport { toolDefinition } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\nimport { createOrder } from './orders'\n\n// Define tool with approval requirement\nconst purchaseItemDef = toolDefinition({\n  name: 'purchase_item',\n  description: 'Purchase an item from the store',\n  inputSchema: z.object({\n    itemId: z.string(),\n    quantity: z.number(),\n    price: z.number(),\n  }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({\n    orderId: z.string(),\n    total: z.number(),\n  }),\n  needsApproval: true,\n})\n\n// Create server implementation\nconst purchaseItem = purchaseItemDef.server(\n  async ({ itemId, quantity, price }) => {\n    const order = await createOrder({ itemId, quantity, price })\n    return { orderId: order.id, total: price * quantity }\n  },\n)\n```\n\nThe user will see an approval prompt showing the item, quantity, and price before the purchase is made. The tool will only execute after the user approves.\n\n## Best Practices\n\n- **Use approval for sensitive operations** - Sending emails, making payments, deleting data\n- **Show clear information** - Display what the tool will do before approval\n- **Provide context** - Show tool arguments in a readable format\n- **Handle denial gracefully** - Check `part.outcome` when a tool is denied.\n- **Timeout handling** - Consider timeouts for approval requests\n\n## Tool Result Outcomes\n\nCancelled and denied tool results keep the existing `state: 'error'` and wire\n`output-error` behavior for compatibility, but `ToolResultPart.outcome` is set\nto `'cancelled'` or `'denied'`. Ordinary tool execution failures do not set an\n`outcome`. The outcome can be produced by the user decision or by middleware\npolicy.\n\nWhen a result is persisted as a `ModelMessage`, the outcome is stored in\n`metadata.tanstack.toolResultOutcome` and restored to the UI part. Consumers\nshould use this structured value instead of matching the fixed error text:\n\n```tsx ignore\nconst denied = message.parts.some(\n  (part) => part.type === 'tool-result' && part.outcome === 'denied',\n)\n```\n\nThe `@tanstack/ai-client` package exposes the same value on the\n`tool-result` part. Use `part.outcome` to distinguish a denial or cancellation\nfrom an ordinary tool execution failure.\n\nThe structured outcome is emitted by both the AG-UI interrupt resume path and\nthe deprecated `addToolApprovalResponse` path. Both paths keep the existing\nerror content.\n\n## Next Steps\n\n- [Server Tools](./server-tools) - Learn about server-side tool execution\n- [Client Tools](./client-tools) - Learn about client-side tool execution\n"}
